      "writtenOn": "2025-11-27T18:12:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just wondering, what exactly happens if this is a virtual function?",
      "revId": "3709bdd70813b5ac8c30ce22c46e5b399fa1059e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e1667495_23e4a1e6",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 46
      },
      "lineNbr": 109,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-01T20:15:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I _guess_ what would happen is: device TLB is not successfully flushed, since the invalidation descriptor is incorrect: it contains the virtual function\u0027s BDF in place of the physical function\u0027s one?\n\nBut since we only get here if the device is in SATC, I guess security-wise we are safe, assuming that we trust the bootloader that all BDFs listed in SATC are BDFs of physical devices, not virtual functions?",
      "parentUuid": "d75c274c_f8459ad9",
      "revId": "3709bdd70813b5ac8c30ce22c46e5b399fa1059e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "67245bdb_a0fcefb9",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 46
      },
      "lineNbr": 109,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-02T10:35:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I guess what would happen is: device TLB is not successfully flushed, since the invalidation descriptor is incorrect: it contains the virtual function\u0027s BDF in place of the physical function\u0027s one?\n\nNice catch. Reading the VT-d spec again, it doesn\u0027t clearly say anything on this error, but invalid descriptor generates a fault reported in IQE(and error record), but the invalidation continues from the next descriptor onwards. \n\n\u003e But since we only get here if the device is in SATC, I guess security-wise we are safe, assuming that we trust the bootloader that all BDFs listed in SATC are BDFs of physical devices, not virtual functions?\n\nYeah, I also didn\u0027t expect virtual functions support yet and assumed only physical functions, but I forgot to put a comment. I think there is no security issue here if we omit this field as this is used as a hint to throttle the number of requests to the device. If we omit this and ats_qdep field, iommu will assume the device can handle upto 32 pending invalidations.\n\nSince a bad value from host cannot tamper security, I guess we can let host pass this value and if its greater than 32, we can unset ats_qdep and pfsid when calling flush_dev_iotlb. 0 value for ats_qdep(MAx invalidation Pending field in the descriptor) makes iommu assume 32. What do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "e1667495_23e4a1e6",
      "revId": "3709bdd70813b5ac8c30ce22c46e5b399fa1059e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c2d441e6_a9fa25d8",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 46
      },
      "lineNbr": 109,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-02T17:54:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Since a bad value from host cannot tamper security\n\nAs long as it doesn\u0027t result, again, in an unsuccessful invalidation, due to the descriptor rejected by hardware.\n\nSo... I guess we _should_ allow the host to set ats_qdep, otherwise we potentially introduce a performance regression? But yeah, it seems a good idea to validate if it is not greater than 32 (aka PCI_ATS_MAX_QDEP).\n\nAs for how exactly we handle an incorrect ats_qdep value:\n\n\u003e we can unset ats_qdep and pfsid when calling flush_dev_iotlb.\n\nWhy not just return -EINVAL instead?\n\n...As for pfsid, it seems we should keep doing what we are doing, i.e. just unconditionally set it to the bdf (and put a comment that we are assuming that all BDFs in SATC are physical functions, not virtual ones)?",
      "parentUuid": "67245bdb_a0fcefb9",
      "revId": "3709bdd70813b5ac8c30ce22c46e5b399fa1059e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d299feb8_811539fe",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 46
      },
      "lineNbr": 109,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-02T18:07:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That said, the question is how _exactly_ we let the host set ats_qdep. This clear_ce hypercall is not the only case when we need an ats_qdep value from the host. Most often we need it for TLB invalidations after DMA unmap, and in that case we are relying on those cache assign/unassign hypercalls to pass ats_qdep. But as I wrote in aosp/3788755: \"we can\u0027t really trust the host to assign the needed cache tags? So pKVM should rather assign the needed cache tags on its own, every time when associating a BDF with an IOMMU domain (when setting a context or PASID entry)?\"",
      "parentUuid": "c2d441e6_a9fa25d8",
      "revId": "3709bdd70813b5ac8c30ce22c46e5b399fa1059e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "69f5f9cf_6cbd6b00",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 46
      },
      "lineNbr": 109,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-02T18:21:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e\u003e we can unset ats_qdep and pfsid when calling flush_dev_iotlb.\n\n\u003e Why not just return -EINVAL instead?\n\nSince it was not security related, I thought we could continue safely without failing the host. But -EINVAL also sounds good.\n\n\u003e we can\u0027t really trust the host to assign the needed cache tags? So pKVM should rather assign the needed cache tags on its own, every time when associating a BDF with an IOMMU domain (when setting a context or PASID entry)?\"\n\nExactly this was what I was going to do as I was working on the flush logic in  aosp/3788755. Host calls `cache_tag_assign_domain` in `dmar_domain_attach_device` after setting context/pasid entry. So I was about to club the cache_tag_assign with context/pasid hypercall. I didn\u0027t have ats_qdep in mind when I was about to code this, but as you said, by having pkvm take over assigning cache tags, we could avoid the cache tag assign hypercalls and have a single point to validate values passed by host.",
      "parentUuid": "d299feb8_811539fe",
      "revId": "3709bdd70813b5ac8c30ce22c46e5b399fa1059e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
